The '''Eurovision Song Contest 1969''' was the 14th edition of the [[Eurovision Song Contest]], held on 29 March 1969 at the {{lang|es|[[Teatro Real]]|i=no}} in [[Madrid]], Spain, and presented by [[Laurita Valenzuela]]. It was organised by the [[European Broadcasting Union]] (EBU) and host broadcaster {{lang|es|[[Televisión Española]]|i=no}} (TVE), who staged the event after winning the {{escyr|1968||1968 contest}} for {{esccnty|Spain|y=1968}} with the song "[[La La La (Massiel song)|La La La]]" by [[Massiel]].

Broadcasters from a total of sixteen countries took part in the contest, with {{Esccnty|Austria}} being the only absence from the seventeen that participated the previous year.

At the close of voting, four entries had received the same number of votes, and were therefore declared joint-winners: {{Esccnty|Spain|y=1969}} with "{{Lang|es|[[Vivo cantando]]|i=no}}" performed by [[Salomé (singer)|Salomé]], the {{Esccnty|United Kingdom|y=1969}} with "[[Boom Bang-a-Bang]]" by [[Lulu (singer)|Lulu]], the {{Esccnty|Netherlands|y=1969}} with "{{lang|nl|[[De troubadour]]|i=no}}" by [[Lenny Kuhr]], and {{Esccnty|France|y=1969}} with "{{Lang|fr|[[Un jour, un enfant]]|i=no}}" by [[Frida Boccara]]. It was the first time in the history of the contest that a tie for first place had occurred, and since the rules in place at the time allowed more than one winner, all four were declared joint winners. France's win was its fourth, thus making it the first country to win the contest four times. The Netherlands' win was its third. Spain and the United Kingdom each won for the second time, with Spain becoming the first country to win the contest twice in a row.

== Location ==
[[File:Teatro Real de Madrid 2025.jpg|thumb|left|{{lang|es|Teatro Real|i=no}}, Madrid – host venue of the 1969 contest.]]
{{lang|es|[[Televisión Española]]|i=no}} (TVE) staged the 1969 contest in [[Madrid]], after winning the {{escyr|1968||1968 contest}} for {{esccnty|Spain|y=1968}} with the song "[[La La La (Massiel song)|La La La]]" by [[Massiel]]. This is the only time that the contest has been held in Spain. The venue selected was the {{lang|es|[[Teatro Real]]|i=no}}, an opera house opened in 1850. After having to close in 1924 due to damage to the building, the venue reopened in 1966 as a concert hall and the main concert venue of the [[Spanish National Orchestra]] and the [[RTVE Symphony Orchestra]].<ref name="Cultural Institutions: Teatro Real">{{cite web | publisher=esMADRID.com | url=http://www.esmadrid.com/en/portal.do?IDM=440&NM=3&TR=C&IDR=653 | title=Cultural Institutions: Teatro Real | access-date=3 September 2009 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090907103422/http://www.esmadrid.com/en/portal.do?IDM=440&NM=3&TR=C&IDR=653 | archive-date=7 September 2009 | url-status=dead }}</ref> At that time, it also housed the [[Madrid Royal Conservatory]] and the [[RESAD|Royal Higher College of Performing Arts]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Teatro Real |url=https://www.spain.info/en/places-of-interest/teatro-real-madrid/ |website=Spain.info |publisher=[[Turespaña]] |access-date=28 December 2023}}</ref>

The press room set up for the event, equipped with a colour [[Eidophor]] projector and a giant screen, twelve television monitors, [[telex]], [[teletype]], and [[telefax]] machines, telephones, one hundred typewriters with different keyboards, translation services, and a radio studio, was located within the building and had capacity for six hundred journalists.<ref name=rdp/><ref>{{cite news |title=Todo a punto |trans-title=Everything ready |url=https://prensahistorica.mcu.es/en/consulta/registro.do?id=11000875423 |newspaper=[[Pueblo (newspaper)|Pueblo]] |date=26 March 1969 |location=[[Madrid]], Spain |page=14 |language=es |via={{ill|Virtual Library of Historical Newspapers|es|Biblioteca Virtual de Prensa Histórica}}}}</ref><ref name=yale>{{cite news |title=Cinco millones y medio de pesetas costará el Festival de Eurovisión |trans-title=The Eurovision Song Contest will cost five and a half million pesetas |url=https://prensahistorica.mcu.es/en/consulta/registro.do?id=11000875372 |newspaper=[[Pueblo (newspaper)|Pueblo]] |date=25 January 1969 |location=[[Madrid]], Spain |page=24 |author=Yale |language=es |via={{ill|Virtual Library of Historical Newspapers|es|Biblioteca Virtual de Prensa Histórica}}}}</ref>

On 27 March 1969, the {{lang|es|Club Internacional de Prensa|i=no}} hosted a cocktail reception at its Madrid facilities, presided over by [[Manuel Fraga]], the [[Ministry of Information and Tourism|Minister of Information and Tourism]] on whom TVE depended at the time, and attended by all the participating artists, executives from the participating broadcasters, numerous accredited journalists, and local authorities.<ref>{{cite news |title=Todo preparado para el Eurofestival-69 |trans-title=Everithing ready for Eurofestival-69 |url=https://prensahistorica.mcu.es/en/consulta/registro.do?id=10005076598 |newspaper={{ill|El Periódico Mediterráneo|es|lt=Mediterráneo}} |date=28 March 1969 |location=[[Castellón de la Plana]], Spain |page=6 |language=es |via={{ill|Virtual Library of Historical Newspapers|es|Biblioteca Virtual de Prensa Histórica}}}}</ref>

== Participants ==
{{further|List of countries in the Eurovision Song Contest}}
{{ESC 1969 participants}}
Broadcasters from sixteen countries participated in the 1969 contest. Of the seventeen countries that participated in 1968 only {{Esccnty|Austria}} was absent,<ref name=ESC1969>{{cite web|title=Eurovision Song Contest 1969|url=https://www.eurovision.com/eurovision-song-contest/madrid-1969/|publisher=[[European Broadcasting Union]]|access-date=19 May 2026}}</ref> officially because {{lang|de|[[ORF (broadcaster)|Österreichischer Rundfunk]]|i=no}} (ORF) could not find a suitable representative,<ref>{{cite news|author=<!--Staff writer(s); no by-line.-->|title=Eurovisionsfest ohne Österreich|url=https://www.arbeiterzeitung.at/ausgabe/19690115a/9|work=Arbeiter-Zeitung|location=Vienna, Austria|date=15 January 1969 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201115123919/http://www.arbeiter-zeitung.at/cgi-bin/archiv/flash.pl?year=1969&month=1&day=15&page=09&html=1 | archive-date=15 November 2020 | url-status=live |access-date=7 May 2023}}</ref> but it was rumoured that the broadcaster refused to participate in a contest staged in [[Francoist Spain|Franco-ruled Spain]].<ref name=History>{{cite book|last=O'Connor|first=John Kennedy|year=2005|title=The Eurovision Song Contest: The Official History}}</ref>

Several of the performing artists had previously represented the same country in past editions: [[Simone de Oliveira]] had represented {{Esccnty|Portugal|y=1965|t=Portugal in 1965}}; [[Kirsti Sparboe]] had represented {{Esccnty|Norway|y=1965|t=Norway in 1965}} and {{Esccnty|Norway|y=1967|t=in 1967}}; and [[Louis Neefs]] had represented {{Esccnty|Belgium|y=1967|t=Belgium in 1967}}. In addition, [[Siw Malmkvist]] representing {{Esccnty|Germany|y=1969}} had represented {{Esccnty|Sweden|y=1960|t=Sweden in 1960}}; and [[Romuald Figuier|Romuald]] representing {{Esccnty|Luxembourg|y=1969}} had represented {{Esccnty|Monaco|y=1964|t=Monaco in 1964}}.

