== Production and format ==
[[File:Medaille Euro Songfestival 1969, Bestanddeelnr 922-2167.jpg|thumb|Eurovision Song Contest 1969 winner's medallion.{{efn|The medallions the winners received were made of silver by a jeweler in Barcelona, measured {{convert|10|cm}} in diameter and weighed {{convert|370|g}}.<ref>{{cite news|title=Las medallas del Eurofestival|magazine=Tele Radio|number=589|date=7 April 1969|page=21|language=es}}</ref>}}]]
The event was presented by [[Laurita Valenzuela]], who was dressed for the occasion by {{ill|Carmen Mir|es}}.<ref>{{Cite magazine|url=https://www.revistavanityfair.es/lujo/moda/articulos/carmen-mir-disenadora-espanola-historia-moda-desfile-nasa-anos-sesenta/36072|title=Carmen Mir, the designer forgotten by history who had 200 employees|date=February 2, 2019|author=Parga, Mónica|magazine=[[Vanity Fair (magazine)|Vanity Fair]]|language=es}}</ref> The [[surrealist]] artist [[Salvador Dalí]] designed the poster for the contest. The musical director was [[Augusto Algueró]], who made the arrangements and conducted the 52-piece orchestra during the opening and ending acts.<ref name=jerez>{{cite news|url=https://www.diariodejerez.es/television/Medio-siglo-rara-victoria-Salome-Eurovision-video_0_1353764943.html|title=Medio siglo de la rara victoria de Salomé|newspaper={{ill|Diario de Jerez|es}}|author=Sempere, Antonio|date=12 May 2019|language=es}}</ref> Each competing delegation was allowed to appoint its own conductor to lead the orchestra in the performance of its entry, with the host musical director also conducting for those which did not bring their own conductor. The event was overseen on behalf of the [[European Broadcasting Union]] (EBU) by [[Clifford Brown (director)|Clifford Brown]] as [[Executive supervisors of the Eurovision Song Contest|scrutineer]].<ref name=ESC1969/> The draw to determine the running order took place in late 1968 in [[London]].<ref name=yale/>

The event had a budget of around 5 million [[Spanish peseta|pesetas]] (€30,050).<ref name=rdp>{{cite news |title=Rueda de prensa sobre el Eurofestival 69 |trans-title=Press conference on Eurofestival 69 |url=https://prensahistorica.mcu.es/en/consulta/registro.do?id=11000875378 |newspaper=[[Pueblo (newspaper)|Pueblo]] |date=1 February 1969 |location=[[Madrid]], Spain |page=12 |language=es |author=Álvarez, Jesús | author-link=Jesús Álvarez (journalist) |via={{ill|Virtual Library of Historical Newspapers|es|Biblioteca Virtual de Prensa Histórica}}}}</ref> For the set built on the {{lang|es|Teatro Real|i=no}} stage, the TVE scenography divisions in Madrid and Barcelona, headed by Bernardo Ballester, designed ten different proposals and built a scale model for each of them. These were shortlisted into three designs for further consideration, from which one was selected. The final design included the 5,000-pipe fixed organ already present at the back of the stage, a central steel sculpture created by surrealist artist {{ill|Amadeo Gabino|es}},{{efn|TVE moved the sculpture, which is about {{convert|5|m}} tall and weighs {{convert|350|kg|abbr=on}}, to the garden at its premises in {{lang|es|[[Prado del Rey (studios)|Prado del Rey]]|i=no}} after the contest, where it has remained ever since.}} a scoreboard on the side, and fifteen thousand red and pink carnations. Since they were not allowed to make any modifications to the stage, not even hammering a nail into it, the set was prefabricated in more than three hundred modules that, brought from {{lang|es|[[Prado del Rey (studios)|Prado del Rey]]|i=no}}, were assembled on-site. For better visibility, the floor of the set was raised {{convert|1|m}} above the stage.<ref>{{cite news |title=Dificultades para el decorado del Teatro Real como escenario del Festival de Eurovisión |trans-title=Difficulties in the decoration of the Teatro Real as the stage for the Eurovision Song Contest |url=https://prensahistorica.mcu.es/en/consulta/registro.do?id=10005076595 |newspaper={{ill|El Periódico Mediterráneo|es|lt=Mediterráneo}} |date=25 March 1969 |location=[[Castellón de la Plana]], Spain |page=6 |language=es |via={{ill|Virtual Library of Historical Newspapers|es|Biblioteca Virtual de Prensa Histórica}}}}</ref> For technical tests of colour broadcasting and lighting, a full-size replica of the {{lang|es|Teatro Real|i=no}} stage with the set was built at Studio 1 in {{lang|es|Prado del Rey|i=no}}.<ref>{{cite news|title=El escenario del Real, reproducido|magazine=Tele Radio|number=586|date=17 March 1969|page=16|language=es}}</ref>

TVE had full access to the concert hall starting from the evening of 16 March 1969, immediately after the audience and staff of that day's regular season concert had left the venue, which allowed its stage crew to begin assembling the set right away, its technical crew to begin setting up the equipment afterwards, and the orchestra to start rehearsals on 22 March.<ref>{{cite AV media|url=https://www.rtve.es/play/videos/programas-y-concursos-en-el-archivo-de-rtve/toda-plana-preparativos-del-xiv-festival-eurovision-1969/5094479/|date=27 March 1969|title=A toda plana - Preparativos del XIV Festival de Eurovisión 1969|type=Television programme|location=[[Madrid]], Spain|publisher={{lang|es|[[Televisión Española]]|i=no}} (TVE)|language=es}}</ref> The rehearsals of the competing delegations on stage with the orchestra took place on 26 and 27 March, eight each day following the established running order for the competition. A full dress rehearsal was held on the evening of 28 March which included the voting process with simulated votes and a tie for first place.{{efn|name=dress}} Technical rehearsals were held on the morning of 29 March, followed by a dress rehearsal in the afternoon ahead of the live broadcast that evening.<ref>{{Cite news|title=Los últimos preparativos para el festival de Eurovisión|trans-title=The final preparations for the Eurovision Song Contest| url=https://prensahistorica.mcu.es/en/consulta/registro.do?id=11001092972 |newspaper=[[Diario Palentino]] |date=13 March 1969 |location=[[Palencia]], Spain |page=10 |language=es |via={{ill|Virtual Library of Historical Newspapers|es|Biblioteca Virtual de Prensa Histórica}}}}</ref>

This was the second contest to be filmed and transmitted in colour, even though TVE did not have the required colour equipment for such a big event at the time. It had to rent such equipment from the German [[ARD (broadcaster)|ARD]], which was provided by [[Fernseh]] and brought to Madrid from Cologne.<ref>{{cite news |title=Aus der Wirtschaft |access-date=11 June 2024 |url=https://archive.org/details/funkschau-1969-heft-10/page/870/mode/2up |work={{ill|Funkschau|de}} |date=15 May 1969 |location=[[Haar, Bavaria|Haar]], [[West Germany]] |page=870 |via=Internet Archive}}</ref> Five colour television cameras inside the concert hall, three of them mounted on cranes, were used to broadcast the event.<ref name=com>{{cite news |title=Ha finalizado la instalación del sistema de comunicaciones del Eurofestival |trans-title=The installation of the Eurofestival communications system has been completed |url=https://prensahistorica.mcu.es/en/consulta/registro.do?id=11000757977 |newspaper=[[Libertad (newspaper)|Libertad]] |date=25 March 1969 |location=[[Valladolid]], Spain |page=7 |language=es |via={{ill|Virtual Library of Historical Newspapers|es|Biblioteca Virtual de Prensa Histórica}}}}</ref> To avoid interruptions in the broadcast, the television signal was transmitted to the other broadcasters feeding the [[Eurovision (network)|Eurovision network]] by two simultaneous ways:{{efn|Except for the connection with [[Lisbon]] for {{lang|pt|[[Radiotelevisão Portuguesa]]|i=no}} (RTP), which used a direct link since it is located in the opposite direction.<ref name=com/>}} via the main terrestrial microwave relay link network through France, and via the [[Intelsat III F-2]] satellite through Italy as a backup.{{efn|To send the backup signal from {{lang|es|[[Prado del Rey (studios)|Prado del Rey]]|i=no}} to the {{lang|it|[[Radiotelevisione italiana]]|i=no}} (RAI) studios in [[Rome]], the satellite ground stations in [[Buitrago del Lozoya]] and [[Fucino Space Centre|Fucino]] were used.<ref>{{cite news|title=El Eurofestival, por dos caminos, transmitido a todo el continente|trans-title=The Eurofestival, by two paths, transmitted to the whole continent|magazine=Tele Radio|number=588|page=26|date=31 March 1969|language=es}}</ref>}}<ref name=rdp/> In addition, 137 dedicated telephone lines were used for transmission, internal communication, and communication with the participating broadcasters.<ref name=com/> In Spain itself the broadcast was seen in black and white because the local transmitters did not support colour transmissions. The colour recording equipment did not arrive in Madrid on time, so TVE only had a black and white copy of the contest, until a colour copy was discovered in the archives of [[NRK]].<ref>{{Cite web|date=2019-03-29|title=50 años de Eurovisión 1969 {{!}} La final de Eurovisión 1969: por primera vez a todo color y con los comentarios de Uribarri|trans-title=50 Years of Eurovision 1969 {{!}} The 1969 Eurovision final: in full color for the first time and with Uribarri's commentary|url=https://www.rtve.es/television/20190329/final-eurovision-1969-primera-vez-todo-color-comentarios-uribarri/1912900.shtml|access-date=2021-05-23|website=[[RTVE]]|language=es}}</ref>

== Contest overview<span class="anchor" id="Results"></span><span class="anchor" id="Participants and results"></span> ==
<!-- Anchors in the header are the old section names -->
[[File:Lenny Kuhr 1969 Eurovision dress.jpg|thumb|upright|[[Lenny Kuhr]]'s dress]]
The contest was held on 29 March 1969 at 22:00 [[Central European Time|CET]]. The show opened with a rendition of the [[Te Deum (Charpentier)|Eurovision tune]] by the {{lang|es|Teatro Real|i=no}} organ, followed by the orchestra performing the previous year's winning song, "La, la, la". The interval act consisted of a surrealist documentary titled {{lang|es|La España diferente}}, directed by [[Javier Aguirre (director)|Javier Aguirre]], with music by [[Luis de Pablo]]. The show ended with the orchestra performing a medley of previous Eurovision winning songs during the credits.<ref name=jerez/>

The winners were: {{Esccnty|Spain|y=1969}} with "{{Lang|es|[[Vivo cantando]]|i=no}}", written by Aniano Alcalde and María José de Cerato, and performed by [[Salomé (singer)|Salomé]]; the {{Esccnty|United Kingdom|y=1969}} with "[[Boom Bang-a-Bang]]", written by Alan Moorhouse and [[Michael Julien|Peter Warne]], and performed by [[Lulu (singer)|Lulu]]; the {{Esccnty|Netherlands|y=1969}} with "{{lang|nl|[[De troubadour]]|i=no}}", written by {{ill|David Hartsema|nl}} and [[Lenny Kuhr]], and performed by Kuhr herself; and {{Esccnty|France|y=1969}} with "{{Lang|fr|[[Un jour, un enfant]]|i=no}}", written by [[Eddy Marnay]] and [[Emil Stern|Émile Stern]], and performed by [[Frida Boccara]].<ref name=scb/>

It was the first time that the contest resulted in a tie for first place, with the four entries each gaining 18 votes. Since the rules in place at the time allowed more than one winner,{{efn|name=dress|In the dress rehearsal held on 28 March, in which voting was also rehearsed with simulated votes, there had already been a tie for first place between Monaco and Luxembourg, both with 18 votes. EBU scrutineer [[Clifford Brown (director)|Clifford Brown]] declared them joint winners after consulting the rules and verifying that there could be more than one winner. Laurita Valenzuela concluded the rehearsal by bidding farewell until next year in Monaco and Luxembourg "at the same time".<ref>{{Cite news|title=Ayer, ensayo general|trans-title=Yesterday, dress rehearsal| url=https://prensahistorica.mcu.es/en/consulta/registro.do?id=11000875426 |newspaper=[[Pueblo (newspaper)|Pueblo]] |date=29 March 1969 |location=[[Madrid]], Spain |page=30 |language=es |via={{ill|Virtual Library of Historical Newspapers|es|Biblioteca Virtual de Prensa Histórica}}}}</ref>}} all four were declared joint winners ''ex aequo''.{{efn|name=tie|Following protests from participating broadcasters after the four-way tie, a tiebreaker rule to determine a single winner was adopted for the first time in the {{escyr|1970||1970 contest}}.<ref>{{cite web|title=Eurovision Song Contest 1970|url=https://eurovision.tv/event/amsterdam-1970|publisher=[[European Broadcasting Union]]|access-date=19 August 2025}}</ref>}} This caused a problem concerning the medals that were to be distributed to them, as there were not enough to go round, so only the singers received their medals on stage;<ref name=ESC1969 /> the songwriters were not awarded theirs until later.<ref>{{Cite news |date=4 April 1969 |title=Por qué hubo medallas para todos los ganadores del Festival de Eurovisión |trans-title=Why were there medals for all the Eurovision Song Contest winners |url=https://prensahistorica.mcu.es/en/consulta/registro.do?id=11000488756 |newspaper=[[Diario de Burgos]] |location=[[Burgos]], Spain |page=12 |language=es |via={{ill|Virtual Library of Historical Newspapers|es|Biblioteca Virtual de Prensa Histórica}}}}</ref> The medals were presented by previous year's winner Massiel, after which all four winning songs were reprised.

